  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "**Control of** and **control over** both mean power over something: *control of the company*, *control over prices*. A person **is in control of** a situation but **under the control of** someone else.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "The police took the control of the building.",
      "correct": "The police took control of the building.",
      "note": "In the fixed phrases **take control**, **lose control**, and **gain control**, there is no **the**."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "The doctor did a control of my blood pressure.",
      "correct": "The doctor checked my blood pressure.",
      "note": "**Control** as a noun does not usually mean an examination; use **check** or **checkup**."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": null,
    "grammar": "\"control\" in its main senses is uncountable and is used without **the** in *take control*, *lose control*.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": "In many languages the word that looks like **control** means *check* or *inspection*. In English the noun usually means *power over something* (*take control*), so *a control of my blood pressure* is wrong; say *a check*.",
